<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  A partial Info.plist, merged into the one `GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE = YES`
	  builds. Everything else this app declares is still a build setting; this
	  file exists for the one key Xcode's generator does not know.

	  `INFOPLIST_KEY_UIBackgroundModes = audio` is accepted as a build setting,
	  survives `-showBuildSettings`, and never reaches the built Info.plist —
	  the generator has a fixed list of keys it understands and this is not one
	  of them. It failed completely silently, and what it would have cost is the
	  whole feature: without the audio background mode iOS suspends this app the
	  moment the user switches to the app they are writing in, which is the only
	  moment dictation is ever used. `Scripts/prove-dictation.sh` check 2 reads
	  the built plist, not the build setting, for exactly that reason.

	  The recording session is also the *only* thing this mode is for. Nothing
	  here plays audio.

	  **A session does not close itself, and this comment used to say it did.**
	  It described a `SharedStore.dictationSessionMinutes` setting (5, 15 or 60,
	  and "deliberately no never") as the bound on how long this app may keep
	  recording. That setting never had a control the user could reach, and as of
	  2026-08-18 it is deleted: all three starters open a session with
	  `DictationService.noSessionLimit`. A session ends when the user stops it,
	  when the audio session is interrupted, or when this process dies.

	  That is a deliberate product decision, not drift: dictation is something
	  you start and stop, and a recording cut off mid-sentence is the worse
	  failure. It is written here because this is the file that justifies the
	  entitlement, and the honest justification is now "the user starts a
	  recording, sees it running, and ends it" rather than "it times out". If App
	  Review asks why this app records in the background, that is the answer, and
	  `DictationService.noSessionLimit` carries the rest of the reasoning.
	-->
	<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
	<array>
		<string>audio</string>
		<!--
		  `fetch` is what lets iOS wake this app to renew the session token that
		  every cloud action in the keyboard depends on. Only the containing app
		  can attest — the keyboard is a different bundle ID and an extension has
		  no `UIApplication` to launch anything with — and a token lasts ninety
		  days, so without this the recovery for an install that has gone stale is
		  to ask the user to open an app they have no reason to open, about a
		  cloud model they have never been shown. See `AppAttestation.scheduleRefresh`.
		-->
		<string>fetch</string>
	</array>
	<!--
	  Submitting a `BGTaskRequest` for an identifier that is not in this list
	  throws, and registering a handler for one that is not in it traps. The
	  string is spelled once in Swift, as `AppAttestation.refreshTaskIdentifier`,
	  and this is the copy iOS reads.
	-->
	<key>BGTaskSchedulerPermittedIdentifiers</key>
	<array>
		<string>com.nitai.aikeyboard.attest</string>
	</array>
	<!--
	  The deep link that lets a keyboard extension hand off to this app so the
	  user can start a dictation session. Registered here so the keyboard can
	  record the intent and the app picks it up on launch.
	-->
	<key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
			<array>
				<string>aikeyboard</string>
			</array>
			<key>CFBundleURLName</key>
			<string>com.nitai.aikeyboard</string>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
